## Part 1: Do we only use git add for new files?
No. This is a very common misconception.
You must use git add for both brand-new files AND files you have previously uploaded but just modified.

* Git does not automatically watch your files live.
* Think of git add like packing a cardboard box. If you edit your text file tomorrow, or write a new pattern in your notebook, you have to run git add to place those updated versions inside the box.

------------------------------
## Part 2: Does git push directly save changes globally?
git push does upload everything globally to GitHub, but it cannot do it alone. It requires a middle step called git commit.
Here is how the workflow actually moves your code:

   1. git add (The Stage): You choose which files/changes go into your "box".
   2. git commit (The Lock): You tape the box shut and write a label on it (your message). This saves it locally on your computer.
   3. git push (The Delivery): You ship that locked box over the internet to your global GitHub repository.

If you don't commit, git push will have nothing to send!
