## Part 3: The Complete Lifecycle Sequence of Git Commands
Here is the exact order of every major Git command from the day you start a project to your daily routine:

## IMPORTANT

## Phase 1: Creation (Only Done ONCE per project)

* git init
* What it does: Creates a hidden .git folder. It turns a regular folder on your computer into a local Git repository.
* git remote add origin <URL>
* What it does: Links your local folder to your online GitHub repository address.

## Phase 2: The Daily Routine (Done EVERY day)

* git status
* What it does: Shows you a list of files you modified or created (they will appear in red text).
* git add . (or git add filename)
* What it does: Stages your changes. (Turns the files from red text to green text).
* git commit -m "Your message"
* What it does: Permanently saves a snapshot of those changes on your hard drive with a description.
* git push
* What it does: Sends your local commits over the internet to your public GitHub profile.

